    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-119361",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ec62fd08-5729-5dc4-97b9-1a422f70109e",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1+({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =2.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
