    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65225",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ec62fd08-5729-5dc4-97b9-1a422f70109e",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t & ={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$",
