    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65222",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ec62fd08-5729-5dc4-97b9-1a422f70109e",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "easy",
