    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65243",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3332b534-bd04-5e86-995e-1e6d691d8e1b",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the trigonometric expression ${sin}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{4}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor ${cos}^{2}⁡x$  from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{4}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1$ to simplify the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+{sin}^{2}⁡x & ={cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{4}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$",
