      "math_academy_id": "e-3329",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ec62fd08-5729-5dc4-97b9-1a422f70109e",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 x$ and $\\cos^2 x$ terms. \n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x -(\\sin^2x + \\cos^2 x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{x} + \\cos^2{x} = 1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n2\\sin x\\cos x - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin x(2\\cos x - \\sin x) - \\cos^2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x - 1.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
