      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying a Trigonometric Expression Using a Common Denominator and the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the second term using the identity $\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\dfrac{\\cos{\\theta}}{\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & = \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin{\\theta}}{1+\\cos{\\theta}}+\\cot{\\theta} = \\csc\\theta.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
